{
  "slug": "radagast",
  "decklist_sha256": "231d89abca22a6a3b6d9f98414f36a325342344ffecef6dfa1df1e3b49c612d2",
  "meta": {
    "space": "embeddings_ability.npy",
    "projection": "classical MDS + SMACOF, canonically oriented",
    "clustering": "average-linkage agglomerative on cosine distance, 2 levels",
    "cards": 71,
    "unresolved": [],
    "note": "Positions are LOCAL to this deck and are not atlas positions — the deck is re-laid-out from its own cards, which is the whole point. Anything that draws this must say so.",
    "named_by": "deck-cartographer",
    "cartographer_notes": [
      "Four cities (city-2, city-4, city-5, city-6) have exactly one neighbourhood whose membership is IDENTICAL to the parent city. That is not a split, so there is nothing narrower to name and I have left all four unnamed. A renderer should suppress a single identical hood rather than print its fallback word under the city label — 'Interaction' set beneath THE TRAPS reads as two different places.",
      "city-0 is the deck's mass at 23 of 71 entries and it is heterogeneous by construction: the commander, all four mass-pump finishers, all four token-makers and all the aura and land ramp sit together. The city name names the job the mass performs — build wide, then cash it — and the real distinctions live in its three neighbourhoods, which is where I spent the naming.",
      "city-0-hood-0 is honest for eight of its ten cards: four finishers (Craterhoof Behemoth, Overwhelming Stampede, Pathbreaker Ibex, Return of the Wildspeaker) and four cards that buy one back (Bala Ged Recovery, Eternal Witness, Life from the Loam, Satyr Wayfinder). Harmonize and Deathgorge Scavenger are the loose members — neither pays off nor rebuys.",
      "city-0-hood-2's loose members are Collector Ouphe, Mirkwood Pathmaker and Dawn's Light Archer: cheap green bodies with no ramp text at all, which is why its fallback word 'Ramp' fits them worse than the name does. Dawn's Light Archer at least has flash printed on it, so it belongs to the window if not to the engine.",
      "city-3-hood-1 is Toski, Bearer of Secrets alone. The cluster is not wrong — he is the only card in the insurance city that protects himself rather than something else — but functionally he is city-6's business, a draw engine keyed to connecting. He is a card that belongs to two cities, not a misfile; I named the hood for the difference the map can actually see.",
      "city-1-hood-1 is Tireless Provisioner alone. The 8-versus-1 split is thin but real: every other member taps for mana, and this one makes Treasures off land drops. Worth a name, not worth a territory.",
      "city-2 carries verified_count 0 despite being the entire removal suite. Ohran Frostfang — the strategic frame's flagship flash trap — is filed in city-6 instead, because its text draws cards, and the verified stack that features it (002) therefore credits A CARD PER BODY rather than THE TRAPS. If the Coach says 'the traps' he means city-2; if he cites stack 002 he is pointing at city-6. Worth knowing before three voices use the word.",
      "No membership was changed and none was renamed around. THE TRAPS and THE BODYGUARDS are both interaction-shaped names and could be confused in the abstract; on the map they are opposite corners (city-2 at -0.73,-0.54 and city-3 at -0.81,+0.36), and the distinction is real: city-2 answers the table, city-3 answers what the table does to you."
    ]
  },
